1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,400
those born poor die poor, welcome to this new analysis, today we dive

2
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:10,279
headfirst into an economic mystery that has always divided public opinion. The question

3
00:00:10,279 --> 00:00:15,480
is simple, but the answer is complex. In our economic system, does everyone really have

4
00:00:15,480 --> 00:00:20,719
the same probability of achieving success regardless of where they start, or is our

5
00:00:20,719 --> 00:00:25,879
financial destiny practically already written at birth? Well, today we will set

6
00:00:25,879 --> 00:00:30,719
aside personal opinions and let the data speak to understand once and for all if

7
00:00:30,719 --> 00:00:36,439
equal opportunities are a solid reality or just a beautiful illusion. There is a brilliant

8
00:00:36,439 --> 00:00:42,679
quote from 1966, often attributed to John Steinbeck, which says: poor workers see

9
00:00:42,679 --> 00:00:47,640
themselves as temporarily embarrassed millionaires. It is a perfect quote because it captures a

10
00:00:47,640 --> 00:00:52,799
huge cultural contrast. On one side is the classic American dream, the almost romantic idea

11
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:57,600
that with elbow grease and determination you can overcome any obstacle, and that poverty is

12
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:02,520
just a minor temporary setback. On the other side, there is the old European view, accustomed to

13
00:01:02,520 --> 00:01:06,640
historically much more rigid social classes, where wealth is often seen as a

14
00:01:06,640 --> 00:01:11,560
closed circle reserved for those who inherited privileges. But the real question is, what do

15
00:01:11,560 --> 00:01:17,360
the real numbers tell us? Section 1, the American dream exists. To understand this, we rely on a

16
00:01:17,359 --> 00:01:24,319
rigorous economic study from 2018, signed by Alberto Alesina, Stefanie Stantcheva, and Edoardo Teso.

17
00:01:24,319 --> 00:01:30,319
The beating heart of all their research is a concept called intergenerational mobility.

18
00:01:30,319 --> 00:01:36,359
What is it? Simply put, it is the probability that a child born in the poorest 20% of the

19
00:01:36,359 --> 00:01:42,200
population manages as an adult to climb the social ladder and reach the highest income

20
00:01:42,280 --> 00:01:48,439
brackets. This is exactly the unequivocal yardstick used by economists to determine

21
00:01:48,439 --> 00:01:55,359
if the rules of the game are fair for everyone. Section 2, the reality of mobility, and here hold

22
00:01:55,359 --> 00:02:00,760
tight, because the raw, hard facts reveal a crazy paradox. If we think of the United States

23
00:02:00,760 --> 00:02:05,879
as the land of absolute opportunity, we have to think again. America has an actual

24
00:02:05,879 --> 00:02:11,120
social mobility that is lower than in much of Europe. The probability of being born poor

25
00:02:11,120 --> 00:02:18,960
and staying stuck in poverty is 33.1% in the United States. In Sweden, it stops at 26.7%.

26
00:02:18,960 --> 00:02:24,800
In essence, the country that invented the concept of the self-made man now finds itself with a social rigidity

27
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:30,800
even higher than the much-criticized European model. But there is a specific number, a single

28
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:37,560
data point in this study that is truly mind-blowing: 7.8%. This is the actual, tiny

29
00:02:37,560 --> 00:02:42,240
probability that a poor child in the United States manages to make the big leap, going from the

30
00:02:42,240 --> 00:02:48,520
absolute bottom up to the richest 20% of the population. We are talking about less than 8 chances

31
00:02:48,520 --> 00:02:55,199
in a hundred of performing the economic miracle. Section 3, optimism versus pessimism. So far we have

32
00:02:55,199 --> 00:03:00,360
talked about real data, but the real twist in this analysis comes when we compare the

33
00:03:00,360 --> 00:03:05,500
reality with what people believe is happening, and a deep psychological split

34
00:03:05,580 --> 00:03:11,659
emerges. In the United States, people tend to wear rose-colored glasses. There is an exaggerated optimism and people believe

35
00:03:11,659 --> 00:03:16,780
in the American dream much more than the numbers justify. Crossing the ocean to Europe,

36
00:03:16,780 --> 00:03:22,139
the exact opposite happens. There is a cosmic pessimism. People are convinced that escaping

37
00:03:22,139 --> 00:03:27,180
poverty is an almost impossible feat, far more difficult than the actual statistics

38
00:03:27,180 --> 00:03:32,780
prove. Let's look exactly at how all this translates. In the United States,

39
00:03:32,780 --> 00:03:39,259
people are convinced that almost 12 children out of a hundred, to be exact 11.7%, will make it

40
00:03:39,259 --> 00:03:45,780
from the bottom to the top, but as we have just seen, the real figure is only 7.8%. French

41
00:03:45,780 --> 00:03:51,900
citizens, on the other hand, think that over 35% of those born poor will stay poor forever. In

42
00:03:51,900 --> 00:03:57,740
reality, the figure stops at 29.2%. In short, our collective beliefs live in a

43
00:04:57,740 --> 00:04:02,939
sort of parallel world, completely disconnected from economic reality. But who are these

44
00:04:02,939 --> 00:04:09,180
incurable optimists? The researchers neutrally isolated the demographic and

45
00:04:09,180 --> 00:04:14,939
ideological traits of those who see the glass half full. They found that this optimism toward the social

46
00:04:14,939 --> 00:04:20,939
elevator is strongly linked to certain characteristics: having right-leaning political views,

47
00:04:20,939 --> 00:04:26,220
being deeply convinced that poverty stems mostly from a lack of personal effort,

48
00:04:26,700 --> 00:04:34,220
not having a university degree, and belonging to younger age groups. Section 4, the role of effort. This

49
00:04:34,220 --> 00:04:39,180
leads us to a crucial reflection on the value of sacrifice. How much does rolling up your

50
00:04:39,180 --> 00:04:43,900
sleeves really matter? The researchers found a practically universal opinion, shared in all the analyzed

51
00:04:43,900 --> 00:04:49,420
countries: almost everyone agrees on one point, working your butt off is definitely useful, but only

52
00:04:49,420 --> 00:04:54,140
up to a point. Hard work helps you escape absolute poverty and gain a place

53
00:04:54,139 --> 00:04:58,779
in the middle class, but there is strong disillusionment if we look at the absolute peak: very few believe that

54
00:04:58,779 --> 00:05:03,019
effort alone is enough to enter the circle of the super-rich if you start from the bottom step,

55
00:05:03,019 --> 00:05:07,500
as if at some point the social elevator needed a special key to go to the top

56
00:05:07,500 --> 00:05:14,699
floor. Section 5, the experiment on perception. And here we are at the most fascinating moment of the entire

57
00:05:14,699 --> 00:05:20,459
paper, a real field experiment. The researchers wanted to find out what happens when

58
00:05:20,459 --> 00:05:25,739
people are put face to face with the harsh and often depressing reality of data. They did

59
00:05:25,739 --> 00:05:31,259
it in three simple steps. First, they surveyed the participants' initial beliefs. Second,

60
00:05:31,259 --> 00:05:37,019
they showed them a short animation that slapped them in the face with the unfiltered, pessimistic data on the

61
00:05:37,019 --> 00:05:42,219
actual, extremely low social mobility. Third, they asked the participants again what kind of

62
00:05:42,219 --> 00:05:47,339
government intervention they desired at that point. They wanted to see if discovering the truth would change

63
00:05:47,340 --> 00:05:53,900
people's minds. The result is the perfect mirror of our polarized society. Faced with the

64
00:05:53,900 --> 00:05:59,740
exact same, irrefutable numbers, people reacted in diametrically opposed ways

65
00:05:59,740 --> 00:06:05,180
depending on their ideology. Those who were left-leaning, seeing how stuck the social

66
00:06:05,180 --> 00:06:10,060
elevator was, reacted by immediately demanding greater government intervention to

67
00:06:10,060 --> 00:06:15,980
restore equal opportunities. Those who were right-leaning, on the other hand, perfectly understood and accepted

68
00:06:15,980 --> 00:06:21,100
the new negative data, but did not request in any way more help or redistribution by

69
00:06:21,100 --> 00:06:25,740
the state. To understand this reaction from the right, which at first glance might seem

70
00:06:25,740 --> 00:06:31,180
counter-intuitive, let us borrow a very explanatory quote from J.D. Vance. "It's not

71
00:06:31,180 --> 00:06:36,620
your fault if you're a loser, it's the government's fault." This thought makes us realize that not wanting state aid

72
00:06:36,620 --> 00:06:41,819
does not mean ignoring the problem at all. Simply, for this school of thought, the

73
00:06:41,819 --> 00:06:46,860
state is not the solution to economic inefficiencies. In fact, it is perceived as the main cause,

74
00:06:46,860 --> 00:06:51,659
so why ask for help from the one you believe is creating the problem? Analyzing instead in

75
00:06:51,659 --> 00:06:56,699
detail the requests that came from the left, we notice a very specific approach. There was no

76
00:06:56,699 --> 00:07:01,500
generic demand for blank checks. What they loudly demanded was more funding for

77
00:07:01,500 --> 00:07:07,420
public education, higher spending on healthcare, and taxes on large inheritances. In practice,

78
00:07:07,420 --> 00:07:12,699
a toolkit aimed at intervening at the starting line of life. The goal is not

79
00:07:12,699 --> 00:07:18,100
to guarantee that everyone reaches the exact same finish line, but to make sure that everyone, and I emphasize

80
00:07:18,100 --> 00:07:24,220
everyone, has exactly the same running shoes at the start. If we were to summarize the essence

81
00:07:24,220 --> 00:07:30,180
of this incredible investigation in a single sentence, it would be this: ideology is a powerful

82
00:07:30,180 --> 00:07:35,420
cognitive filter. We never look at economic data neutrally; we constantly filter them

83
00:07:35,420 --> 00:07:39,900
through our political lens. Even when numbers debunk our most cherished cultural myths,

84
00:07:39,900 --> 00:07:45,180
our political alignment does not make us ignore the facts, but inexorably shapes the solution

85
00:07:45,180 --> 00:07:50,819
we are willing to accept. The exact same data simply leads to different recipes. And all

86
00:07:50,819 --> 00:07:56,420
this work leaves us with one final question, one of those that really make us think

87
00:07:56,420 --> 00:08:01,939
deeply. When we clash with the evidence that the wheel of the economy does not turn as we would like,

88
00:08:01,939 --> 00:08:08,219
are we, as a society, really looking for a way to guarantee everyone a concrete chance,

89
00:08:08,219 --> 00:08:14,100
or are we using our political lenses—whether steeped in wild optimism or in

90
00:08:14,100 --> 00:08:20,579
total distrust of the state—to defend and justify the status quo? A fundamental question,

91
00:08:20,579 --> 00:08:27,300
and perhaps the key to understanding how and if we will ever decide to rewrite the rules of the game. See you at the next

92
00:08:27,300 --> 00:08:27,699
analysis.